  "draftIsFinal": false,
  "summary": "I wrote src/main.ts with the water scene, and it passes typecheck. I couldn't run it in a browser or build it, so the shader compile and the final look are untested.\n\n- **Waves:** The water is a 300×300 grid, 500 units wide, with four summed sine waves displacing it in the vertex shader. The normals come from the analytic slope of those waves. A `waveClock` uniform is set every frame from `onFrame`.\n- **Fragment shader:** Procedural fbm value noise perturbs the normal for ripple detail, and the ripple fades with distance. A Schlick fresnel mixes the deep/shallow water colour with the reflected sky. The reflection uses the same sky gradient as the background. A sharp plus broad sun specular glint is added on top, and distant water blends into the horizon colour.\n- **Sky:** `sky.gradient` with the zenith `#2f6fbf` and horizon `#b9d8f0`. The shader repeats those colours as linear constants, so the reflection only approximately matches the engine's sky.\n- **Colour:** The shader outputs linear light. The engine's output stage handles tone mapping and sRGB encoding, so I kept its default tone mapping and didn't apply gamma in the shader.\n- **Camera:** At (0, 4, 20), looking toward the horizon at 50° field of view.\n- **Ready flag:** `__EVAL_READY__` is set two animation frames after `app.ready()` resolves. I avoided top-level `await` because there is no Vite config, so the default build target may reject it.\n\nTwo things could go wrong. I assumed `waveClock` is auto-declared in the fragment stage as well as the vertex stage, which the docs only show for the vertex stage. If it isn't, the fragment shader fails to compile and the draw is skipped silently. Also, I left the sun out of the engine's sky because I couldn't confirm its azimuth convention. The glint therefore has no matching sun disc in the sky.",
